     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered it. A confirmation alone isn't enough for me to place an order at 5 times the maximum dose.\n\nSertraline 1000 mg/day is far above any approved or commonly used dose. People who confirm a flagged dose quickly sometimes meant to confirm what they *intended* (often 100 mg) rather than what was typed. If this goes to the patient as written, it could cause serious harm.\n\n**I'll enter it right away if you tell me either of these:**\n\n1. **The corrected dose.** For example, \"sertraline 100 mg PO daily.\"\n2. **The reason for 1000 mg/day and who approved it.** For example, a psychiatry recommendation, an approved protocol or a pharmacist review. I'll put that in the order note so pharmacy and nursing can see it.\n\nThe pharmacist reviewing the order will almost certainly question an unexplained 1000 mg sertraline order anyway. Adding the reason now will keep the order from being delayed."
      }
